#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# prime_pi

#% text
# `prime_pi(x)` gives the number of primes at most `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `x` may be any real number; `prime_pi(x)` is 0 for `x < 2`.

#% item
# The count uses a sieve for small `x` and the Meissel-Lehmer method for larger `x`.

#% item
# `prime_pi` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
prime_pi(10)
#% output
#= 4

#% cell
prime_pi(100)
#% output
#= 25

#% cell
prime_pi(1000.5)
#% output
#= 168

#% cell
prime_pi(1)
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Large arguments:

#% cell
prime_pi(10^10)
#% output
#= 455052511

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
prime_pi([10, 100, 1000, 10000])
#% output
#= [4, 25, 168, 1229]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `prime_pi` counts what `prime` enumerates:

#% cell
prime_pi(prime(500))
#% output
#= 500

#% text
# The prime number theorem, `x/log(x)` against the count:

#% cell
[prime_pi(10^8), 10^8/log(1.0e8)]
#% output
#= [5761455, 5428681.023790647]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `prime`, `next_prime`, `log_integral`, `is_prime`
